        "id": "alg-ip-prior-preserving-crank-nicolson",
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "Prior-preserving Crank–Nicolson sampler",
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $H$ be a separable real Hilbert space, let $\\mu_0$ be a centered Borel Gaussian probability measure on $H$, let $D\\subseteq H$ be Borel with $\\mu_0(D)=1$, and let $\\Phi_D:D\\to(-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be Borel. Assume the evidence $$ Z:=\\int_D e^{-\\Phi_D(u)}\\,\\mu_0(du) $$ satisfies $0<Z<\\infty$. Extend the potential to all of $H$ by setting $\\bar\\Phi(u)=\\Phi_D(u)$ for $u\\in D$ and $\\bar\\Phi(u)=+\\infty$ for $u\\notin D$, and define the target probability $\\mu(du)=Z^{-1}e^{-\\bar\\Phi(u)}\\mu_0(du)$. For a scale $\\beta\\in[0,1]$, write $\\rho=\\sqrt{1-\\beta^2}$. At state $u\\in H$, draw $\\xi\\sim\\mu_0$ independently and propose $v=\\rho u+\\beta\\xi$. Set the acceptance probability to $0$ if either $\\bar\\Phi(u)$ or $\\bar\\Phi(v)$ is $+\\infty$, and otherwise set $$ \\alpha(u,v)=\\min\\{1,\\exp(\\bar\\Phi(u)-\\bar\\Phi(v))\\}. $$ Draw an independent uniform random variable on $[0,1]$ and move to $v$ if it is at most $\\alpha(u,v)$; otherwise remain at $u$. The resulting one-step transition rule is the prior-preserving Crank–Nicolson, or pCN, rule $P_\\beta$. States with infinite potential are absorbing under this total definition; they have $\\mu$-measure zero.",
