        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and the inputs of the prior-preserving Crank–Nicolson algorithm: a separable real Hilbert space $H$, centered Borel Gaussian reference probability $\\mu_0$, full-mass Borel domain $D$, Borel potential $\\Phi_D:D\\to(-\\infty,+\\infty]$, positive finite evidence $Z$, and scale $\\beta\\in[0,1]$. Let $\\mu$ be the normalized target on $H$ obtained by extending $\\Phi_D$ by $+\\infty$ off $D$, and let $P_\\beta$ be the algorithm's accept-reject transition. Then $P_\\beta$ is reversible with respect to $\\mu$: its joint flow $$\\Lambda_\\beta(C):=\\int_H P_\\beta(u,C_u)\\,\\mu(du),\\qquad C_u:=\\{v\\in H:(u,v)\\in C\\},$$ is unchanged by $(u,v)\\mapsto(v,u)$ for every Borel $C\\subseteq H\\times H$. Consequently, $\\mu$ is invariant: $$\\int_H P_\\beta(u,A)\\,\\mu(du)=\\mu(A)\\qquad(A\\in\\mathcal B(H)).$$ This conclusion gives invariance only; it does not assert uniqueness, convergence, mixing, an effective-sample-size bound, mesh-independent cost, or correctness of an approximate forward solver.",
